TY  - JOUR
TI  - Nucleic acid recognition during prokaryotic immunity.
AB  - Parasitic elements often spread to hosts through the delivery of their nucleic acids to the recipient. This is particularly true for the primary parasites of bacteria, bacteriophages (phages) and plasmids. Although bacterial immune systems can sense a diverse set of infection signals, such as a protein unique to the invader or the disruption of natural host processes, phage and plasmid nucleic acids represent some of the most common molecules that are recognized as foreign to initiate defense. In this review, we will discuss the various elements of invader nucleic acids that can be distinguished by bacterial host immune systems as "non-self" and how this signal is relayed to activate an immune response.
