TI  - Self-replicating DNA circuit for high-fidelity in situ imaging of T4 polynucleotide kinase activity in living cells.
AB  - Polynucleotide kinase (PNK) is a pivotal enzyme in nucleic acid metabolism and genome maintenance, and its intracellular activity is closely associated with various disease processes. However, existing methods for monitoring PNK activity in living cells are often hindered by limited sensitivity, operational complexity, and insufficient spatial resolution. Here, we present a self-replicating DNA circuit (SDC) for in situ imaging of intracellular PNK activity with high sensitivity and spatial accuracy. The SDC comprises two functional modules: a primary signal transduction module, in which PNK-mediated phosphorylation and λ-exonuclease digestion release a trigger strand (T) from a hairpin probe; and a secondary signal amplification module, where T initiates a self-replicating hybridization chain reaction (SDC). This circuit produces branched DNA nanostructures through self-replicating assembly, generating recombinant initiators that sustain the amplification loop. The resulting high-molecular-weight assemblies exhibit limited diffusivity, enabling localized signal enrichment and enhanced imaging performance. This enzyme-free, isothermal amplification strategy offers a powerful tool for visualizing PNK activity in live cells, with potential applications in studying DNA repair dynamics and developing nucleic acid-based diagnostic platforms.
