TI  - A flexible electrochemical biosensor based on virus-templated gold nanowires for sweat glucose monitoring.
AB  - Nanowires hold great potential for improving signal stability in flexible electrochemical devices, yet the fabrication of micron-scale nanowires with uniform morphology and high purity remains technically challenging. Here, virus-templated gold nanowires (AuNWs) were synthesized using genetically engineered M13 (G5-M13) phages and employed for flexible electrode fabrication in wearable glucose sensing. The highly ordered major coat protein (pVIII) of the filamentous M13 phage enabled the production of AuNWs with uniform dimensions and high purity (97.57 %). When integrated onto a screen-imprinted electrode (SPE), the resulting G5-M13-AuNWs formed a flexible, entangled three-dimensional conductive network that maintained stable electrochemical performance under repeated deformation. The G5-M13-AuNWs/SPE sensor enabled sweat glucose detection with a linear range of 0.02-3 mM and a detection limit of 7 μM. A pilot study showed strong correlation with blood glucose measurements before and after meals. The sensor maintained 94.5 % response after 50 bending/stretching cycles and showed no skin irritation after 12 h wear. Compared to sensors fabricated with commercial gold nanowires, our device exhibited better flexibility and signal stability, which is attributed to the entangled architecture of the virus-templated nanowires. These findings highlight the potential of virus-templated nanowires as functional materials for deformable electrodes and pave the way for their application in reliable, non-invasive, and real-time monitoring of physiological biomarkers.
