TI  - Linking fluorescence signatures of dissolved organic matter to fecal contamination dynamics: A rapid assessment strategy for urban river systems.
AB  - Conventional methods for assessing fecal contamination are slow and hinder rapid response. This study integrated fluorescence quantitative polymerase chain reaction (qPCR), three-dimensional excitation emission matrix fluorescence spectroscopy (3D-EEM) and parallel factor analysis (PARAFAC) to investigate fecal contamination and dissolved organic matter (DOM) in the Liuxi River Basin in China. The results showed that human-associated Bacteroidales marker (BacHum) and bacteriophage marker (CPQ_064) dominated contamination patterns, exhibiting strong spatial-seasonal variability with significantly higher downstream concentrations during wet seasons (p < 0.001). Notably, these markers showed robust correlations with tryptophan-like DOM components (p < 0.001), suggesting a co-occurrence and shared environmental behavior between tryptophan-like components and fecal contamination markers. Microcosm experiments further confirmed a significant positive correlation between tryptophan-like components and human-associated fecal markers (BacHum and CPQ_064) (p < 0.001), with tryptophan-like DOM components accounting for 62.40 % of fecal-derived DOM. This validated tryptophan-like fluorescence as a cost-effective indicator of fecal contamination, reducing analysis costs by 90 % (2.00 USD→ 0.20 USD/sample) and time by 96 % (6 h → 15 min/sample). Moreover, first-order (BacHum) and biphasic (CPQ_064) decay models further elucidated marker persistence in aquatic environments. These results reinforced the establishment of DOM fluorescence signatures as rapid indicators of fecal contamination dynamics and enabled further inference of the primary sources of fecal contamination in water bodies. This work provided a field-deployable framework for real-time contamination tracking, enabling targeted mitigation strategies in urban riverine systems.
