TY  - JOUR
TI  - Characterization of the phage ΦK64 depolymerase S2-4 and its therapeutic effect against K1 serotype Klebsiella pneumoniae.
AB  - The infection rate and drug resistance of Klebsiella pneumoniae containing capsular polysaccharides (CPSs) have been increasing annually, resulting in severe human and animal infections. Depolymerases derived from bacteriophages can degrade CPSs and thus hold potential for treating bacterial infections. However, little is known about the mechanism by which K. pneumoniae phage depolymerases hydrolyze CPSs. In this study, the S2-4 encoded by the phage ΦK64 was identified as a potent depolymerase against K1 serotype Klebsiella CPSs. Cryo-electron microscopy structural analysis revealed that S2-4 forms a homotrimer with a spindle-like structure comprising a particle-binding domain, a core receptor-binding domain, an insertion domain, and a C-terminal domain. The results of structural assays suggest that S2-4 possesses multiple catalytic centers, which may contribute to its potent depolymerase activity. S2-4 inhibited K. pneumoniae biofilm formation, disrupted preformed biofilms, and enhanced macrophage adhesion and phagocytosis in depolymerase-treated bacteria. In a murine model, a single dose of 5 µg of S2-4 provided full protection against bacterial infection, underscoring the potent depolymerase activity of S2-4. These results indicate that S2-4 is a potent depolymerase against K1 serotype Klebsiella CPSs and has the potential to be a promising candidate for the clinical control of K. pneumoniae infections.
