TI  - Antibacterial activity of single Klebsiella phage and phage cocktail against clinical carbapenem-resistant Klebsiella pneumoniae in vitro.
AB  - Klebsiella pneumoniae is a gram-negative, facultative anaerobic bacillus, that represents a significant global health concern due to its role as a major pathogen in multidrug-resistant infections. The growing prevalence of carbapenem-resistant K. pneumoniae infections among human necessitates the development of alternative and complementary therapies. Phages can effectively lyse drug-resistant pathogenic bacteria. In this study, stability tests confirmed that phage P560 was stable over a wide range of pH (3 to 10) and temperatures (4 to 50 °C) conditions. The growth characteristics showed that the optimal MOI of P560 was 0.00000001, the latent time period of phage P560 was 20 min, the burst period was 20-70 min, the lytic cycle was 70 min, and the burst size was about 131 phages/cell. In vitro, the number of viable cells in three phage-treated groups (MOI = 0.1, 0.01, 0.001) decreased by 4.25 log10 CFU/ml, 4.20 log10 CFU/ml and 4.07 log10 CFU/ml, respectively, after 80 min of incubation, in comparison to that in the untreated group. Besides, phage cocktail composed of P560 and other phages can effectively inhibit the mixture of KL64 and KL47 type carbapenem-resistant K. pneumoniae within 5 h. The spray of phage cocktail could achieve the sterilization rate of 99.92 ± 0.1% in a closed space and 93.13 ± 1.6% on the floor, respectively. In conclusion, phage P560 showed considerable antibacterial activity in vitro.
