TI  - The current situation and perspectives of bacteriophage-derived endolysins for controlling foodborne pathogens and their biofilms in food industry.
AB  - Food safety challenges arising from foodborne pathogens and their biofilms necessitate innovative antimicrobial strategies. Bacteriophage endolysins, which degrade bacterial cell walls by cleaving peptidoglycan bonds, offer a targeted solution for food safety applications. These enzymes disrupt both planktonic cells and biofilm matrices through enzymatic hydrolysis, addressing limitations of conventional sanitizers used in food processing environments. Mechanistically, endolysins combine enzymatically active domains and cell wall-binding domains to ensure specificity. Their efficacy spans Gram-positive and Gram-negative bacteria, with engineered variants enhancing outer membrane penetration; however, comparative studies reveal that efficacy varies substantially depending on the target pathogen, matrix composition, and delivery system-highlighting a critical gap in standardized benchmarking across food matrices. Broad applicability in meat, dairy, and vegetable sectors is noted, where endolysins reduce pathogen viability and extend shelf life. Economically, advancements in recombinant production and delivery systems improve scalability. Regulatory frameworks require toxicity and immunogenicity assessments, yet endolysins' low propensity for resistance development positions them as sustainable alternatives to antibiotics. This review underscores endolysins' transformative potential in advancing food safety, addressing biofilm-related contamination, and aligning with food industry demands for clean-label solutions.
