TY  - JOUR
TI  - Phage Therapy Beyond Static Pharmaceuticals: A Framework for Controlled Evolutionary Platforms.
AB  - Rising antimicrobial resistance has revived global interest in phage therapy, yet its transition to standard clinical practice remains slow. This challenge is not solely due to a lack of efficacy. Instead, we face a fundamental conceptual barrier caused by an "evaluation mismatch." Traditional regulations treat phages as static chemical molecules-like taking a "snapshot." However, biologically, phages are dynamic, evolving populations-more like a living "movie." In this review, we use Schrödinger's cat metaphor to explain this reality: phage variability is not a defect, but an essential feature. To bridge this gap, we propose a Controlled Evolutionary Platform. By distinguishing between a fixed "Safety Core" and a fluctuating "Adaptive Periphery," we can manage viral evolution rather than trying to stop it. Ultimately, to integrate phages into modern medicine, we must redefine "consistency": shifting our focus from preserving a fixed genetic sequence to ensuring the reliable performance of population dynamics.
